    {
      "id": "sons-i-y-u",
      "title": "Les sons [i], [y] et [u]",
      "minutes": 5,
      "lede": "Three closed vowels that look similar but are produced differently: [i] (spread lips), [y] (rounded lips, tongue forward) and [u] (rounded lips, tongue back).",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Trois voyelles fermées",
          "headers": [
            "Son",
            "Description",
            "Exemples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "[i]",
              "souriant : lèvres étirées",
              "merci · Clichy · difficile"
            ],
            [
              "[y]",
              "arrondi, aigu : langue en avant",
              "Lulu · bienvenue · la rue"
            ],
            [
              "[u]",
              "arrondi, grave : langue en arrière",
              "bonjour · beaucoup · les courses"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "How to hear it",
          "text": "- To make [y]: say [i] (smile), then round your lips as if for [u] without moving your tongue. This is the sound in tu, rue, une, sur, vu, bien sûr.\n- [u] is spelled ou (bonjour, nous, tout, vous); [y] is spelled u (tu, nu, sur); [i] is spelled i or y (ici, si, y, style). Never say tu as “too” and ou as “you”.\n- Minimal pairs: tu [ty] / tout [tu] / thé; rue [ʁy] / roue [ʁu] / ri; vu [vy] / vous [vu] / vie [vi]. Practise changing only lip shape.\n- English speakers often say “oo” [u] for [y]: tu → too. Fix: tongue forward, lips tight round."
        },
